        <div id="diagnose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is Your Basement Wall Wet From Condensation or Seepage?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tape a square of plastic to the wall for a day or two and see which side gets wet: droplets on the room side mean condensation, and droplets against the masonry mean seepage. Use clear polyethylene about 18 inches on a side and seal all four edges tightly to bare wall. This is the approach behind ASTM D4263, the standard moisture check that coating manufacturers cite, and it settles the most important question before you spend a dollar.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does moisture on the room side mean?</h3>

          <p>
            Beads on the outer face of the plastic come from warm, humid basement air condensing against a cool wall. A sealer cannot cure that, because the water never passed through the concrete in the first place. A <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier</a>, better air movement and insulation are the right tools for this version of the problem.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does moisture behind the plastic mean?</h3>

          <p>
            Moisture trapped between the sheet and the wall is wicking through the masonry from the soil outside, and that is exactly the situation a wall sealer is designed to handle. Other signs point the same way. A dark band along the lowest foot of the wall, crusty white salt deposits, or a crack that weeps after a heavy rain all mean water is sitting in the soil against the foundation.
          </p>

          <p>
            A steady trickle, or water spurting through a tie hole or crack, belongs in a different category altogether. That is active water under pressure, and it has to be plugged and drained before any coating stands a chance of staying on the wall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does the timing of the dampness tell you anything?</h3>

          <p>
            It does. Condensation is worst in humid summer weather, usually on the coolest stretch of wall, and it fades once the furnace starts running. Seepage follows rain and snowmelt, appears a day or two after a storm and hangs around at the base of the wall. Jot down what the wall looks like after rain and after a muggy dry spell for a few weeks, and the pattern tends to reveal itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What should you write down before you shop?</h3>

          <p>
            Three facts: whether the wall is poured concrete or hollow block, whether it is bare or carries old paint, and the worst behavior you have seen, on a scale from damp to seeping to running. With those answers in hand, the seven picks above shrink to one or two in about a minute, and you avoid buying a coating for a problem that really calls for a shovel.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Wet air needs a dehumidifier. Wet masonry needs a sealer.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">When the plastic test shows water on the room side, put your budget into dehumidification first. A wall sealer can only help with moisture that gathers behind the sheet.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Types of Basement Wall Sealer Are There?
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are four families, and each one does a different job: cement-and-latex masonry waterproofers, penetrating sealers, elastomeric liquid rubbers and crystalline slurries. Knowing which family a can belongs to tells you more about how it will behave on your wall than the brand name or the marketing on the label.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does a masonry waterproofer do?</h3>

          <p>
            Products such as DRYLOK are thick coatings packed with fine solids. Scrubbed firmly into the surface, they plug the pores of block and concrete and leave a film that resists water pushing from behind. They change the look of the wall because they go on like paint, and they bond only to bare, clean masonry. For an interior block wall that seeps, this is the family to start with.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do penetrating sealers differ?</h3>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers, a group that includes silicate products like RadonSeal and silane-siloxane water repellents, soak into the concrete and react beneath the surface. There is no film, so nothing can peel, and the wall looks the same afterward. Silicates densify concrete by filling its capillaries, while silane-siloxanes make the walls of the pores shed water. Both need an unpainted surface they can soak into.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When does a liquid rubber make sense?</h3>

          <p>
            Elastomeric coatings such as Liquid Rubber and Ames Block and Wall cure into a stretchy membrane, and flexibility is the reason to buy one: they can span hairline cracks that open and close with the seasons. They do their best work on the outside of a foundation, or on interior walls that are damp rather than under real pressure, because a rubber film forced off the wall from behind will bubble.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is crystalline waterproofing?</h3>

          <p>
            Crystalline products, sold under names like Xypex, are cement slurries carrying reactive chemicals that grow crystals inside the concrete whenever water is present. It is a professional-grade system used on tanks and below-grade structures. It suits poured concrete, demands a saturated surface and careful mixing, and tolerates shortcuts far less than a can of masonry paint does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which type fits your wall?</h3>

          <p>
            Film coatings suit walls you will look at and want dry. Penetrants suit walls you want to keep as bare concrete. Elastomerics suit walls that crack and move. Crystalline slurries suit poured walls that deserve a permanent, professional treatment. In practice, most owners of block foundations end up with a film coating, and most owners of sound poured walls do well with a penetrant.
          </p>

