        <div id="pressure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Does a Hydrostatic Pressure Rating Mean on a Wall Sealer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It is the water pressure a film coating was built to withstand from behind in the maker's test, and DRYLOK lists 10 psi for Original and 15 psi for Extreme. Water in saturated soil presses harder on a foundation the deeper it goes, adding roughly 0.43 psi for every foot of water above a given point. With the water table 8 feet up the wall, the base sees about 3.5 psi. Ratings of 10 or 15 psi therefore look generous, but they come from a lab test on a sample, not from your wall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which sealers carry a rating?</h3>

          <p>
            Only the film coatings built to stay bonded while water pushes behind them. DRYLOK Original lists 10 psi and DRYLOK Extreme lists 15 psi. Elastomeric interior coatings usually have no rating at all, and penetrating sealers cannot have one, since there is no film to push off. Use the figure to break a tie between two film coatings rather than reading it as a guarantee.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does the rating leave out?</h3>

          <p>
            More than it covers. No wall coating stops water that enters at the seam between the slab and the wall, known as the cove joint. None holds back water pouring through a crack, and none affects water rising up through the slab. If water appears in any of those places, drainage is the main fix and the wall coating plays a supporting role at best.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers on the Labels</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
