          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How much rating does your wall need?</h3>

          <p>
            A wall that is damp, or that seeps slowly after storms, is well within reach of any carefully applied masonry waterproofer. A wall with running water makes the rating irrelevant. Sort out grading, gutters and cracks first, then coat. Our step-by-step guide to <a href="/how-to-waterproof-a-basement/">waterproofing a basement from the outside in</a> lays out that order in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Basement Wall Sealer Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plan on 18 to 24 gallons of masonry waterproofer for two coats on 1,000 square feet of wall, far more than wall paint, because the first coat disappears into the pores. Measure the wall, not the floor: multiply the basement's perimeter by the height of bare wall, then subtract large openings. A 30-by-40-foot basement has 140 feet of perimeter, and at 7.5 feet of exposed wall that comes to about 1,050 square feet. Walkouts with partial-height walls, or basements with a finished section, bring the number down quickly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How far does a gallon of masonry waterproofer go?</h3>

          <p>
            DRYLOK lists roughly 75 to 100 square feet per gallon for the first coat on smooth concrete, and less on rough or split-face block. The second coat spreads further because the pores are already filled. For the 1,050-square-foot example, budget 11 to 14 gallons for the first coat and 8 to 10 for the second.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What about penetrating sealers and liquid rubber?</h3>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers depend on how thirsty the concrete is. Dense poured walls may take 150 to 200 square feet per gallon, and open-textured block can soak up twice as much. Each maker publishes a range, and the sensible approach is to buy for the low end of it and return any unopened containers. Elastomeric coatings go on thicker and usually come in around 50 to 75 square feet per gallon per coat.
          </p>

