        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Prepare a Basement Wall for Sealer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Get the wall down to clean, bare, sound masonry, with no loose paint, no salt, no mold and no open cracks. A masonry coating grips masonry and nothing else, so scrape away loose paint and then wire-brush every square foot. The prep is the dull part of the job, and it is also the part that decides whether the coating is still on the wall in five years.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you coat over old paint?</h3>

          <p>
            Usually not if you want the rated performance. Even well-bonded existing paint keeps most masonry waterproofers from performing as labeled, and the label typically calls for bare surfaces. Penetrating sealers are stricter still, because they cannot soak through a film at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you deal with efflorescence?</h3>

          <p>
            Efflorescence is the white powder or crust that mineral salts leave behind as water evaporates out of the wall. Brush it off dry, wash with a masonry cleaner or a mild acid etch made for concrete, and rinse thoroughly. Coating over it is the most common reason a basement coating flakes off within a year: the salts keep growing beneath the film and push it away from the masonry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which cracks need patching first?</h3>

          <p>
            Every one of them, before any coating goes on. The coating itself can fill hairline cracks if you work it in with the brush. Anything wider than about 1/8 inch needs a masonry patch or a polyurethane crack sealant. An actively leaking hole calls for hydraulic cement, which expands as it sets and can stop a trickle within minutes. Let each patch cure for as long as its directions say before coating over it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What conditions does the coating need?</h3>

          <p>
            Clean off any mold with a product labeled for the job, let the wall dry thoroughly and check the forecast. Most coatings need surface and air temperatures above 50 degrees Fahrenheit throughout the cure. A dehumidifier running for a few days before and after application shortens dry times and keeps humid basement air from fogging the fresh film.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Do You Apply a Masonry Waterproofer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stir it to the bottom of the can, brush the first coat hard into the masonry, let it dry for the time on the label, add a second coat and then hunt down pinholes with a work light. None of those steps is difficult, but skipping any one of them is how a coating rated for 10 or 15 psi ends up leaking at a fraction of that.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does stirring matter so much?</h3>

          <p>
            Stir it; never shake it. The solids in a masonry waterproofer settle hard, and an under-mixed can lays down a thin, watery first coat that never reaches its rated performance. Mix thoroughly and stir again every 15 minutes as you work. Boxing several gallons together in a five-gallon pail keeps color and consistency even across the whole wall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Brush or roller for the first coat?</h3>

          <p>
            A brush, every time. Use a stiff nylon masonry brush and drive the coating into each pinhole and mortar joint. Work in small sections, scrub the material in, then lay it off in one direction, and move on only when the finished patch shows no pinholes. It is slow going, and it is exactly where the performance comes from.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When does the second coat go on?</h3>

          <p>
            Once the first coat has dried for the time printed on the label, usually a few hours. A thick-nap roller is acceptable for this coat on smooth poured concrete; on block, stay with the brush. Afterward, hold a work light flat against the wall. Any pinhole that glows is a path for water, so dab it shut.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you handle pipes, boxes and the sill?</h3>

          <p>
            Cut in around pipes, electrical boxes and the sill plate with a smaller brush, and carry the coating a few inches onto the slab at the foot of the wall so the cove joint gets covered instead of missed. Mask the wooden sill and any framing you want to keep clean. Masonry waterproofer is hard to get off wood once it sets, and it was never meant to protect lumber in the first place.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What safety steps does the job need?</h3>

          <p>
            Keep air moving for the whole job, and take extra care with solvent-based products in a basement: open the windows, run a fan blowing outward, and think about pilot lights and the furnace if the label warns of flammable vapors. Water-based products are easier to live with, yet they still give off vapor as they cure. Allow at least the full recommended cure before stacking boxes or framing against the wall.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">PINHOLE CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Block always needs a second pass.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Hollow concrete block is full of tiny voids, thousands of them. Brush on the first of two full coats, and inspect the finished wall under a raking light before you clean the tools.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="inside-outside" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Should You Seal a Basement Wall From the Inside or the Outside?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Outside is the better engineering, inside is what most homeowners can afford, so fix the outside cheaply and seal the inside carefully. Interior coatings sit on what is called the negative side, where water arrives through the soil and the wall and pushes against the coating. Exterior membranes sit on the positive side and meet the water first, so pressure presses them into the wall instead of peeling them away. The price of that advantage is digging, which is why exterior work costs so much more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which cheap outside fixes come first?</h3>

          <p>
            Extend downspouts several feet from the foundation, regrade so soil slopes away from the house (residential building code calls for a drop of 6 inches over the first 10 feet where possible), and keep gutters clear. Those three steps cut the amount of water reaching the wall and make an interior coating far more likely to succeed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When is exterior excavation worth it?</h3>

          <p>
            When the soil around the house stays saturated, when the wall is bowing or cracking structurally, or when you are digging anyway for another reason. At that point a professional applies a membrane, adds drainage board and sets a footing drain. If you are weighing that against an interior drain and pump, our ranking of <a href="/best-basement-waterproofing-systems/">basement waterproofing systems</a> puts the two side by side.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is there a middle option for damp block?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes, for block walls that are damp but not leaking: a penetrating silane-siloxane sealer applied from the inside reduces wicking without a film that could blister. If you plan to paint afterward, read our guide to the <a href="/best-paint-for-cinder-block/">best paint for cinder block</a> first, because many penetrating sealers keep paint from sticking.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What if you will finish the basement later?</h3>

          <p>
            Treat the coating as one layer in a wall system rather than the whole answer. Building science guidance for below-grade walls generally prefers rigid foam board against the masonry over fiberglass batts and poly sheeting, because foam keeps the wall surface warm and tolerates some moisture. A sealed, dry wall makes a better base behind that foam than an untreated one, though the coating is not a vapor barrier for a finished room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Much Does It Cost to Seal a Basement Wall?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Doing it yourself typically costs around $300 to $900 in coating for a 1,000-square-foot basement, plus another $50 to $150 for brushes, cleaner and patch material, while hiring it out runs about $3,000 to $7,000. The products are the cheap part. At the time of writing, a gallon of premium masonry waterproofer costs roughly $35 to $60, a five-gallon pail roughly $150 to $250, and a gallon of penetrating sealer roughly $40 to $90 depending on concentration.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What do contractors charge?</h3>

          <p>
            Typical contractor pricing puts a cleaned, patched, two-coat interior application at $3 to $7 per square foot, which is where the $3,000 to $7,000 figure for a full basement comes from. Most of that labor is scrubbing and brushing, and anyone comfortable with a paintbrush and a little patience can handle it over two or three weekends.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How does a sealer compare with a drainage system?</h3>

          <p>
            It is a different, smaller tool, not a budget version of the same thing. An interior perimeter drain with a sump pump commonly costs $5,000 to $15,000, and exterior excavation with membrane work can reach $15,000 to $30,000 or more on a full foundation. Bought for dampness and minor seepage, a sealer is good value; bought to stop a flood, it is money wasted.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How long does the DIY job take?</h3>

          <p>
            Count time as well as money. Coating a full basement yourself usually means a day of clearing and scrubbing, a day of patching and drying, and two separate days of brushing, stretched across a week or more once cure times are included. Penetrating sealers go on faster, often in a single afternoon with a pump sprayer, but the wall still has to be stripped and cleaned first, and that part takes the same effort either way.
          </p>

