        <div id="brands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. How Do DRYLOK, RadonSeal, Liquid Rubber and Xypex Compare?
          </h2>

          <p>
            DRYLOK is the easiest to buy and to get right, RadonSeal is the invisible option for poured walls, the liquid rubbers suit cracks that move, and Xypex is a contractor product. DRYLOK, made by United Gilsonite Laboratories, owns the hardware store shelf for good reason: two clearly separated formulas, published pressure ratings and a data sheet that explains surface prep in plain language. Its weak point is that it works only on bare masonry, which rules it out for many older basements unless they are stripped first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where does RadonSeal fit?</h3>

          <p>
            RadonSeal and similar silicate penetrants are sold mainly online, often direct from the maker, with detailed instructions for testing whether your concrete will absorb the product. Their appeal is invisibility and permanence, because the reaction happens inside the wall. Their limit is hollow block: its thin shells and open cores give the chemistry less material to react with than a solid poured wall does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What should you check on a liquid rubber label?</h3>

          <p>
            Liquid Rubber and Ames attract owners whose cracks keep reopening. Read their labels closely for the recommended dry film thickness and the number of coats it takes to reach it, because a single thin coat of an elastomeric product delivers very little of the flexibility you are paying for. Xypex stands apart from all of them as a commercial product, sold through a distributor network with technical support aimed at contractors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which document matters most before you open the can?</h3>

          <p>
            The technical data sheet, whatever the brand. It gives the surface temperature range, the recoat window, the minimum age of new concrete and whether the product can go over existing coatings. Those four details explain most of the coating failures owners describe online, and every one of them is in print before the first brushstroke.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. When Should You Not Use a Basement Wall Sealer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip the sealer when the wall is moving, when the moisture is condensation, when radon is the real worry, or when the basement floods. Each of those needs a different fix first, and a coating applied too early only hides the evidence or fails within a season.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is the wall cracking or bowing?</h3>

          <p>
            Never coat a wall that is moving. Horizontal cracks in block, stair-step cracks through the mortar joints and inward bowing all point to soil pressure on the structure. A coating covers the evidence for a season while the movement carries on underneath. Have a structural engineer inspect it, because repairs such as wall anchors or bracing have to come first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is the problem a musty smell from condensation?</h3>

          <p>
            If the plastic-sheet test showed condensation, a sealer will not cure the musty smell. That odor comes from humidity and organic material, and the fix is controlling the moisture in the air. Our walkthrough on <a href="/how-to-fix-a-damp-basement/">fixing a damp basement</a> goes deeper into that diagnosis and the order of repairs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can a wall sealer fix radon?</h3>

          <p>
            Not on its own. Some penetrating sealers carry radon in their names, and sealing cracks and slabs can cut down on soil gas entry, but EPA guidance treats sealing as a supplement to active mitigation rather than a replacement for it. Test the air, and if results come in at or above 4 pCi/L, call a certified mitigation contractor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does the basement take in standing water?</h3>

          <p>
            Then a wall coating is the wrong first step. Water that pools after storms is getting in at the floor joint or through the slab, well beyond a wall coating's reach, and the basement needs drainage and a sump. If the walls still feel damp once that is done, coat them then, and the product will last far longer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Common Mistakes With Basement Wall Sealers
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Brushing over efflorescence</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Salt crust left on the wall keeps growing beneath a new film and shoves it off, usually inside the first year. Brush it away dry, wash with a masonry cleaner or concrete etch, rinse well and let the wall dry completely before coating. Salt that returns quickly means water is still moving through the masonry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rolling on the first coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A roller glides over the pores of block rather than filling them, leaving thousands of pinholes behind. The first coat of any masonry waterproofer needs a stiff nylon brush scrubbed into the surface. Keep the roller for the second coat on smooth poured concrete, and check the finished wall under a raking light.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the cove joint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A lot of basement water comes in where the wall meets the slab rather than through the face of the wall. Coat the lowest few inches carefully and seal the cove joint with a product made for it. If water still rises along that seam, no coating will stop it; an interior drain will.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating over old paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most masonry waterproofers and every penetrating sealer need bare masonry to perform as labeled. A new coat over sound latex paint leaves the waterproofer bonded to paint that is bonded to the wall, so the system is only as strong as that old grip. Strip or grind the paint off first, or pick another approach.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing before you fix the gutters</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Moving roof water away from the foundation is the cheapest waterproofing any house can get. Coating the inside of a wall while a downspout empties right beside it is doing the job backward. Extend the downspouts several feet, regrade low spots, and coat the wall only if it is still damp after a few heavy rains.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

