        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which sealer works best on a basement wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a bare block or concrete wall that seeps or stays damp, a cement-and-latex masonry waterproofer such as DRYLOK Extreme is the most reliable option, since it fills pores and carries a published hydrostatic pressure rating. On unpainted poured concrete that you want to look untouched, a penetrating sealer like RadonSeal Plus suits better. Where water actually runs, neither one replaces drainage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will an interior sealer hold back outside water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Up to a point. An interior sealer resists water that has already reached the wall, so it handles dampness and slow seepage best, and that is how most homeowners use one. Heavy, sustained pressure is harder, because the water pushes the coating away from the masonry. Fix the gutters and grading outside first and any interior sealer stands a far better chance of lasting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is one coat of sealer enough on a basement wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not for a masonry waterproofer: most walls need two coats with the first brushed in, and rough or split-face block may need a third pass over stubborn pinholes. Penetrating sealers typically need one saturating coat, sometimes with a second wet-on-wet application on very porous block. Follow the label for your product, because recoat windows change with product and temperature.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why pick DRYLOK over Flex Seal for a whole wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because DRYLOK is built for that job. It is formulated to bond into masonry pores and carries a pressure rating, whereas Flex Seal Liquid is a general-purpose rubber coating better suited to small patches, flashing and exterior spots. Flex Seal can handle a damp-proofing touch-up, but coating an entire foundation wall with it is poor value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a basement wall sealer stop mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only indirectly. By cutting the moisture that wicks through the wall, a sealer removes one source of the dampness mold depends on, and some masonry waterproofers carry a mildewcide in the film. It does nothing about mold that grows where humid air condenses on surfaces. To control mold, hold relative humidity below 60 percent, ideally between 30 and 50 percent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does basement waterproofing paint last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically five to ten years on an interior wall before it needs touch-ups, and sometimes much longer, provided the wall was well prepared and the coating properly applied. How much water reaches the wall matters most. Early failures usually trace back to efflorescence left underneath, a first coat rolled on thin, or gutters and grading that still send water toward the foundation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I paint over a basement wall sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Over a masonry waterproofer, usually yes: use a latex paint once the coating has cured, though many owners skip that step because waterproofers come tinted. Over a penetrating sealer, often no, since silane-siloxane repellents in particular can stop paint from bonding. If paint is part of the plan, pick the sealer with that in mind and confirm compatibility on the data sheet.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

